[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to Xamarin and .NET MAUI Transition Path"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Xamarin has been a foundational cross-platform mobile framework for C# developers for years. However, Microsoft has evolved the ecosystem into .NET Multi-platform App UI (.NET MAUI). This tutorial guides you through understanding Xamarin and planning your transition path to .NET MAUI."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Evolution from Xamarin to .NET MAUI"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":".NET MAUI is the natural evolution of Xamarin.Forms. While Xamarin.Forms allowed developers to build iOS and Android apps from a single shared codebase, .NET MAUI unifies the entire .NET stack, integrating Android, iOS, macOS, and Windows development into a single modernized framework with unified project structures."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences in Project Structure"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Xamarin.Forms, a standard solution typically required three platform-specific projects (.iOS, .Android, and optionally .UWP) alongside the shared project or .NET Standard library. .NET MAUI adopts a single-project multi-targeting system."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"<PropertyGroup>
  <TargetFrameworks>net8.0-android;net8.0-ios;net8.0-maccatalyst</TargetFrameworks>
  <TargetFrameworks Condition="$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('windows'))">$(TargetFrameworks);net8.0-windows10.0.19041.0</TargetFrameworks>
  <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
  <UseMaui>true</UseMaui>
  <SingleProject>true</SingleProject>
</PropertyGroup>"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Step-by-Step Transition Strategy"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Audit your existing Xamarin.Forms solution and identify third-party NuGet packages that need .NET compatible upgrades."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Upgrade your C# projects to target .NET 6 or higher before attempting the final migration to .NET MAUI."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Migrate your App.xaml and initialization logic to use the new MauiProgram builder pattern."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Updating Startup and Dependency Injection"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Xamarin.Forms used various initialization methods inside platform projects. .NET MAUI utilizes a centralized builder approach in MauiProgram.cs, making dependency injection a first-class citizen across all target platforms."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"public static class MauiProgram
{
    public static MauiApp CreateMauiApp()
    {
        var builder = MauiApp.CreateBuilder();
        builder
            .UseMauiApp<App>()
            .ConfigureFonts(fonts =>
            {
                fonts.AddFont("OpenSans-Regular.ttf", "OpenSansRegular");
            });

        return builder.Build();
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Migrating from Xamarin to .NET MAUI ensures your mobile and desktop applications remain supported, performant, and aligned with modern .NET standards. Plan your dependencies carefully, leverage the single-project template, and enjoy the streamlined development experience."}]
